<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grigory Potemkin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1739–1791</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Prince Potemkin, Grigory Aleksandrovich Potemkin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grigory Potemkin was a Russian statesman and field marshal, the favourite and close partner in government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>. As governor of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> he directed the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1783, founded towns such as Kherson and Mykolaiv, and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea-fleet">Black Sea Fleet</a>. He commanded Russian forces in the Russo-Turkish War of 1787–1792, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ochakiv">Ochakiv</a> in 1788, and died during the peace talks in 1791.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grigory-potemkin.jpg" alt="Grigory Potemkin" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Johann Baptist von Lampi the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princepotemkin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
